        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Caramelise the onions and cook the bacon"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Melt half the butter in a wide frying pan over medium-low heat. Add {{onion}} finely sliced onions and a pinch of salt. Cook, stirring every few minutes, for 18 to 20 minutes until soft and golden. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the same pan over medium-high heat, fry the {{streaky-bacon}} of streaky bacon rashers until crisp on both sides. Set aside on a plate."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cook the liver"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Season the {{calves-liver}} of liver slices with salt and pepper on both sides, then dust lightly with {{plain-flour}} of plain flour, shaking off the excess."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Raise the heat to high. Add the remaining butter and the sage leaves to the pan. Once the butter foams and the sage crisps (about 30 seconds), lay the liver slices in the pan."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook for 90 seconds per side. The liver is done when firm at the edges but still slightly pink in the centre. Do not overcook: it should not be grey all the way through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lift the liver and sage onto warm plates. "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Deglaze",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                        "attrs": {
                          "termSlug": "deglaze"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " the pan with {{stock-beef}} of beef stock, scraping up the browned bits. Boil for one minute until reduced by half."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Serve"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spoon the caramelised onions alongside the liver. Lay the bacon over the top. Pour the pan juices over everything."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "symptom": "Liver is tough and grey all the way through",
              "cause": "Cooked for too long, or pan not hot enough so it steamed rather than seared",
              "fix": "The pan must be very hot before the liver goes in. A thin slice needs only 90 seconds per side. Pull it out while the centre is still pink."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Liver smells very strong",
              "cause": "Mature beef liver used instead of calf's liver",
              "fix": "Calf's liver is milder. If using beef liver, soak the slices in milk for 30 minutes before cooking, drain, and pat dry."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Flour coating falls off during cooking",
              "cause": "Flour applied too thickly or liver was wet when dusted",
              "fix": "Pat the liver dry before flouring. Use a light, even dust and shake off the excess."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Liver and onions fell out of fashion in British cooking by the 1980s, mainly because school dinners served it badly: overcooked, grey, and swimming in a thin gravy. Properly cooked calf's liver is a different dish entirely: tender, slightly sweet, with a metallic richness that pairs well with the sharpness of caramelised onion. It remains one of the cheapest pieces of offal at a butcher's counter and one of the most underused."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}